{"causes":["The rise of mass media in the mid-20th century created an appetite for record-keeping and trivia.","The need for authoritative sources to resolve disputes in social settings like pubs led to the creation of the book.","A growing global interest in unique achievements and human capabilities spurred the demand for a compilation of extraordinary records."],"effects":["Guinness World Records popularized the concept of record-breaking, leading to numerous annual attempts and celebrations worldwide.","The publication has become a valuable educational resource, utilized in classrooms to inspire curiosity and achievement.","It has influenced various industries, including marketing and entertainment, by integrating record-setting events into their strategies."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"hugh-beaver","sourceName":"Sir Hugh Beaver","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"guinness-world-records","targetName":"Guinness World Records","context":"In 1955, Sir Hugh Beaver created the concept of Guinness World Records to settle arguments in pubs."},{"sourceSlug":"guinness-world-records","sourceName":"Guinness World Records","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"world-sporting-events","targetName":"World Sporting Events","context":"The Guinness World Records has inspired numerous sporting events and competitions focused on record-breaking since its inception."},{"sourceSlug":"guinness-world-records","sourceName":"Guinness World Records","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"world-records-day","targetName":"World Records Day","context":"Guinness World Records contains the annual celebration known as World Records Day, which encourages global participation in record-setting."},{"sourceSlug":"london","sourceName":"London, England","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"guinness-world-records","targetName":"Guinness World Records","context":"The first edition of Guinness World Records was published in London in 1955."},{"sourceSlug":"norris-mcwhirter","sourceName":"Norris McWhirter","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"guinness-world-records","targetName":"Guinness World Records","context":"Norris McWhirter collaborated with his twin brother to co-found Guinness World Records in 1955."}],"places":[{"name":"London, England","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Dublin, Ireland","role":"Cultural influence"},{"name":"New York City, USA","role":"Major market"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Guinness World Records","type":"Reference article","slug":"guinness-world-records"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q41675","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guinness_World_Records"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Guinness World Records left a notable mark on Contemporary history, documented across 104 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Guinness World Records (originally published as The Guinness Book of Records) is an annual reference book published by Guinness World Records Ltd., first published on 27 August 1955 by the Guinness Brewery as a commercial gift for pub landlords. The first edition was compiled by twin brothers Norris (1925–2004) and Ross McWhirter (1925–1975) after managing director Sir Hugh Beaver was unable to settle a pub argument about whether the golden plover or the grouse was Europe's fastest game bird — the concept was that a book of verified facts about superlatives (the fastest, the tallest, the oldest, the heaviest, the most numerous) could settle similar disputes and serve as a promotional tool for the brewery.

The Guinness Book of Records became one of the bestselling copyright books of all time — it has appeared on The New York Times bestseller list more than any other book, has sold over 143 million copies in more than 100 countries, and has been published in at least 37 languages. Its annual publication (first as a UK edition, expanded globally from the 1960s) made it a fixture of Christmas gift-giving in the English-speaking world — 'looking up records in the Guinness Book' became a cultural ritual that persisted for decades, and the book's role as a family reference and object of wonder established it as one of the most widely owned reference books in history.

Guinness World Records transformed the concept of the 'record' from a competitive sporting achievement into a cultural category — the pursuit of Guinness records became a global participatory phenomenon, generating thousands of record-breaking attempts annually in categories from athletics to eating, from the bizarre to the inspiring. The brand's evolution from printed annual to a global media and events organisation — including record certification, live events, and television specials — demonstrates how a promotional concept became a self-sustaining cultural institution.","subjects":["Reference Books","20th Century","Norris McWhirter","Popular Culture","Record-Breaking","British Culture","Commercial Publishing","Cultural Institutions"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Guinness World Records has shaped global perceptions of achievement and competition, encouraging millions to pursue record-setting endeavors, thus fostering a culture of ambition and creativity.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
